{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 14, "model_name": "mistralai/mixtral-8x7b-instruct-v0.1", "content": " Raymond III Count of Tripoli was a nobleman who played a significant role in the history of the Crusader states in the Levant During his rule from 1152 to 1156 he was involved in various military campaigns and political machinations that would shape the region for years to come\n\nRaymond III was born around 1140 the son of Raymond II Count of Tripoli and Hodierna of Jerusalem He was the nephew of Queen Melisende of Jerusalem and was closely related to the ruling families of the other Crusader states\n\nIn 1152 Raymond III succeeded his father as Count of Tripoli He soon became embroiled in the power struggles of the region When the King of Jerusalem Baldwin III died in 1163 Raymond III supported the claim of his own wife Constance against that of Baldwin's brother Amalric I This dispute led to a civil war in which Raymond III was defeated and forced to flee to Damascus\n\nDespite this setback Raymond III continued to play an important role in the region He allied himself with Nur al-Din the Muslim ruler of Syria and participated in the defense of the Crusader states against the invading forces of Saladin In 1174 Raymond III even led a failed attempt to retake Jerusalem from Saladin\n\nRaymond III died in 1187 during the Battle of Hattin in which the Crusader forces were decisively defeated by Saladin This marked the beginning of the end of the Crusader presence in the Levant\n\nRaymond III Count of Tripoli was a complex and controversial figure who played a significant role in the history of the Crusader states in the Levant He was known for his military prowess and his political acumen but also for his shifting alliances and his ultimate failure to prevent the decline of the Crusader states\n\n*Military career of Raymond III*\n===============\n\nRaymond III was a skilled and experienced military leader who played a key role in the defense of the Crusader states against their Muslim adversaries He is best known for his participation in the defense of the city of Tripoli against the forces of Nur al-Din in 1164 and for his leadership of the Crusader forces during the Battle of Hattin in 1187\n\n*Political career of Raymond III*\n===============\n\nRaymond III was also a shrewd and ambitious politician who was deeply involved in the power struggles of the Crusader states He is best known for his support of his wife Constance's claim to the throne of Jerusalem in 1163 and for his shifting alliances with the Muslim rulers of the region\n\n*Legacy of Raymond III*\n===============\n\nRaymond III's legacy is mixed He is remembered for his military achievements and his political acumen but also for his ultimate failure to prevent the decline of the Crusader states His defeat in the civil war of 1163 and his failure to retake Jerusalem in 1174 are seen as turning points in the history of the Crusader states in the Levant", "logprobs": [{"token": "Raymond", "logprob": -9.107174992095679e-05}, {"token": "III", "logprob": -0.0001829695247579366}, {"token": "Count", "logprob": -0.5162993669509888}, {"token": "of", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "Tri", "logprob": -6.198863957251888e-06}, {"token": "pol", "logprob": -1.1920928244535389e-07}, {"token": "i", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "was", "logprob": -0.13580985367298126}, {"token": "a", "logprob": -0.1442764848470688}, {"token": "noble", "logprob": -0.027132412418723106}, {"token": "man", "logprob": -0.0026453284081071615}, {"token": "who", "logprob": -2.285027027130127}, {"token": "played", "logprob": -0.058923617005348206}, {"token": "a", "logprob": -1.0967194612021558e-05}, {"token": "significant", "logprob": -0.005419088061898947}, {"token": "role", "logprob": -1.1920928244535389e-07}, {"token": "in", "logprob": -0.009756145067512989}, {"token": "the", "logprob": -8.344646857949556e-07}, {"token": "history", "logprob": -0.0009296386269852519}, {"token": "of", "log